  "body": "  The Koszul complex is exact in positive degrees. Every differential is continuous and strict onto its image, and every image is closed. In particular, if then is closed. Moreover, there exists such that There also exists such that    Lean (koszulGraph_restricted_exact)  Lean (koszulDifferential_continuous)  Lean (koszulDifferential_isStrict)  Lean (isClosed_range_koszulDifferential)  Lean (pow_smul_graphIdeal_inter_unitBall_subset)  Lean (pow_smul_ker_d1_inter_subset)   Over the polynomial ring , the sequence has Koszul complex exact in positive degrees. The map is flat: this is the usual identification of with the -adic completion of , followed by inverting . Tensoring the polynomial Koszul complex with , and using the standard base-change identification of its finite free terms, proves positive-degree exactness. Notice that this does not assert exactness in degree zero.  Each differential is continuous because its coordinates are finite sums of multiplication maps. The image of is closed by the noetherian closed-ideal theorem. In higher degree, exactness identifies an image with the kernel of the next continuous differential, and hence that image is closed. The nonarchimedean open mapping theorem, applied to each differential with its closed image, now gives a constant such that every element of the image has a preimage of norm at most times its norm. This proves strictness.  For choose so that , where is a controlled-lift constant for . Scaling a controlled lift of an element of gives . Applying the same argument to , with its own constant and using , gives .   "
