  "body": "  The Huber pair is sheafy.   Lean (isSheafy_JetA_of_dvr)  Lean (finiteJet_isSheafyFor_of_dvr)  Lean (finiteJet_isSheafyTateRing_of_dvr)  Lean (finiteJet_structurePresheaf_isSheafOfTopologicalRings_all_of_dvr)   Give their maximal rings of integral elements. The max\/Gauss estimates show that every structural map is norm-nonincreasing. Consequently it sends bounded subsets to bounded subsets; in particular, if is power-bounded, then the set of powers of its image is the image of the bounded set . The structural maps therefore send power-bounded elements to power-bounded elements and define morphisms of Huber pairs.  Each of is an affinoid -algebra and is therefore sheafy by Huber's theorem . Let in . Push the datum for and the data for the to . If a section over restricts to zero on every , its images over and vanish by separatedness there. Injectivity of the localised Milnor row from then shows that the original section is zero.  Now take a matching family over the . Its images glue over and . The two glued sections have the same image over , since their restrictions agree on the pushed cover and the presheaf for is separated. Exactness of the localised Milnor row over gives a section over , and injectivity of the corresponding rows over the shows that it restricts to the given family.  It remains to retain the topology. The image of the product restriction map is the matching-family equaliser just described, which is closed in the finite product of the section rings. The restriction map is continuous and injective, so the nonarchimedean closed-range theorem, using the topologically nilpotent unit of , makes it a topological embedding. This proves the sheaf condition on finite rational covers. The sheaf-on-a-rational-basis theorem then gives the all-open topological sheaf condition for .   "
